WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Hello, I'm very thrilled
to have a conversation

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.820
with costume designer
Arianne Phillips, who

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.460
is an Academy member
and a three-time Oscar

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.270
nominee for her work on
Walk the Line, W.E. and Once

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.250
Upon a Time in Hollywood.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:15.990
Today we're going to
talk about her work

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.080
specifically on Hedwig
and the Angry Inch.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.960
So Arianne, thanks very
much for joining us.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.900
I am thrilled to have you here.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.140
First thing I want to do
is thank you for the items

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.783
that you've donated to the
Academy's library and museum.

00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:32.700
Would you like to talk
a little bit about what

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.320
some of those things were?

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.950
Sure, thank you Randy for
having me in anticipation

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.180
of the opening of the museum.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:45.210
I am so excited to be part of
anything and everything AMPAS

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.910
museum so I really
appreciate being here.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:52.560
And being able to talk about
one of the most important films

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.440
in my personal trajectory
as a costume designer.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.570
Hedwig is, I would say
probably at the center point

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.230
of the most meaningful--
one of the most

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:08.550
meaningful and sustainably
creative experiences

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:13.680
that keeps on going
as the appreciation

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.830
and the love for Hedwig 20
years later is not lost on me,

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.810
so thrilled to talk about it.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:21.210
And yes.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.040
For many years
I've been donating

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.210
my sketches and visual materials
to the Margaret Herrick Library

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.880
with wonderful Anne
Coco over there,

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:34.080
and it's just been
such a wonderful asset

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:40.800
to be able to have a place where
the work can live on and be

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.230
there if anybody's
interested in looking at it

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.800
and it can be taken care of and
looked after and, of course,

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.090
in the vault of history, of
our own history as filmmakers.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:55.260
So my journey in
terms of donations

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:59.610
really started with that
relationship with Anne Coco.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:03.270
And over the years, I've donated
many sketches, some of which

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.220
are Hedwig sketches.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:10.770
And then when I got news that
the museum would be collecting

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:15.540
specific pieces, costume
pieces, for their archive,

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.460
I think there is a
ripple of excitement

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.390
through our community
and me especially because

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.670
of all the films
that I've designed,

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:27.060
Hedwig happens to be
the only film in which I

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.600
own the costumes which was
part of my initial deal

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:35.100
that I made with the producers
given that it was a small indie

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.850
film.

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:41.230
And it was to offset the
lack of compensation.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:43.200
I said, well, I'll
keep the costumes.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.480
And yeah, I've been
holding on to them.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.070
And here and there people
will come over to my studio

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.040
and look at them
and get so much joy.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.450
You never know when
you're working on a film

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:57.840
how it will resonate
with the audience.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.710
And Hedwig is just
such a special movie

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:05.670
and that generationally
people are responding to it.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:11.780
And it's become a classic in
its own kind of outsider way.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.390
So I guess it was
very fortunate of me

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.970
to be able to make that
deal with the producers

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:22.890
that I could keep the costumes
and now have the opportunity

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:27.480
to donate them to the museum
so that future generations

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.160
and our community, if people
are interested in, can see them

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.590
and someday maybe they'll
make it on exhibition.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:38.670
And the thing about paper
materials and textiles,

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:42.600
costumes, is that they
can deteriorate over time.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.670
Lose their luster, their
color, the quality.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.590
So knowing that
these costumes are

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:53.760
being preserved under the
curation of the museum

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.620
is just thrilling.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.850
Fantastic, thank you
so much for that.

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:01.290
And I just want to say
that these costumes, even

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:04.080
though they happen to
be the only set you have

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.050
from your work, it's a great
set to have because they

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:11.980
are such iconic images and I
think they also are just fun.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.750
There's a lot of times when you
watch a film and the costumes

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:20.010
feel like they're just
part of the natural story

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:22.560
and you're not really paying
that much attention to them,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:26.400
even though they are things
that make an impression

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.140
subconsciously
about the character.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.260
But in the case
of Hedwig, they're

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.370
just such definitive
pieces that immediately

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:38.490
you get a new piece of the
Hedwig personality every time

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:39.870
a costume appears.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.830
And I know you had like
41 costumes for Hedwig

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:44.100
to do in that film.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.870
Just for John Cameron Mitchell.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:46.662
Yeah, there you go.

00:04:46.662 --> 00:04:48.330
There are thousands
of costumes in movie

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.080
but, thank you for saying that.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.460
I mean, I think the thing
about costume design

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.800
is its character development
and character illustration.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:58.740
So the great thing
about Hedwig is

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:02.130
people have a tendency to see
it more than once, like Rocky

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.220
Horror Picture
Show so you know--

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.950
your second time, or maybe
even your third if you're lucky

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:12.870
or fourth, you'll see different
layers of story in there not

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.420
only with the costumes but also
with the wonderful production

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.670
design by Thérèse DePrez

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:22.050
And I think the wonderful thing
about John Cameron Mitchell

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:26.400
as a director is he
really encouraged

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.170
us to create layers of story.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:33.990
And whether it was
something hidden or seen

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:37.830
that would inform us in
the making of the movie.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.650
So yeah, there are
lots of things.

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:44.670
The story is such
a wonderful journey

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:48.600
that you might need to see
the second time to really get

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.240
some of the high moments.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.670
Or 22nd time, it depends.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:57.030
Some people need a lot of
time to soak it all in.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:57.630
So anyway--

00:05:57.630 --> 00:05:58.800
Whatever it takes.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.840
It's a movie that's
fun to re-watch.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:04.080
I know that it's hard to
choose among your children

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.690
when you have 41
children in representing

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:09.330
John's costumes in this film.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:13.510
But at the same time, I
literally gasped when I saw,

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:18.300
for lack of a better term,
the hair or probably better

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.137
to call it the wig dress.

00:06:20.137 --> 00:06:20.970
It's the hair dress.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:22.053
We call it the hair dress.

00:06:22.053 --> 00:06:23.310
The hair dress.

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:27.570
Either way it fits into a nice
double entendre type of thing.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:29.760
I think that the fun
thing about that is you

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.100
can just see the
sheer joy that he's

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.720
having performing in that--

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:34.405
The Terror.

00:06:34.405 --> 00:06:34.905
Yes.

00:06:34.905 --> 00:06:37.050
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.570
There's a fine line between
joy and terror as we all know.

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.700
And that's a dress
too that I think

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:44.070
the craftsmanship
of that dress really

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:47.310
shows, because it moves
in just a particular way

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:50.520
and it just feels
so organic and I'm

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:52.890
sure that was not
the easiest thing

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:55.530
to put together and make work.

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:57.390
Just my own personal
favorite there.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:58.370
Oh, thank you.

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:00.000
It's one of my favorites too.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.790
I mean, if think about
it, John Cameron Mitchell

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:06.840
as the director was directing
every day in full make-up,

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.400
wigs, and high heels.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.390
So why not add a
dress made of a wig.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:13.240
It's too much.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:19.020
So we have some Polaroids that
if somebody saw them laying

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:23.170
on a table might just casually
think they were snapshots

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:23.670
or whatever.

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:26.640
But I'm assuming that they
were actually reference photos

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.130
that you were taking,
and maybe some of them

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:30.930
were for fun too I don't know.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:36.880
But they're really unique
images from that period of work.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:38.190
So let's take a look.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:42.570
Let's do a little slideshow
of some of your own Polaroids.

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:47.490
Yes so one of the things,
I miss Polaroid so much.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.560
I always had a
Polaroid camera with me

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:51.510
on set around this time.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:55.140
And I was just so
marveled, like I said,

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.840
that and John Cameron
Mitchell not only

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.140
was starring as Hedwig, but
he was directing himself.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:05.760
And here he is checking
the shot before he's

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:09.450
in the scene in full wig,
make-up, and costume.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:10.710
And I was just--

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:14.760
he popped up on the dolly that
to check the shot before he ran

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.030
in front of the camera and
I was in constant awe of him

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:19.320
and I just love this shot.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.790
It just made me laugh with
the Chinese daily special

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:25.510
behind him.

00:08:25.510 --> 00:08:27.570
This is from a fitting.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:30.360
This costume became
pretty iconic

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.640
because it was on the
poster for a while.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:38.159
And this is John without,
of course, wig and make-up.

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:40.440
Yeah, one of the things
usually in fittings, it's

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:44.280
always great when the
actors are able to have

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:46.320
the wig on especially
if it's a different hair

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:47.770
color and the make-up.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:50.460
This was, I think,
one of those days

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:54.480
where John being the director
was so over scheduled.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.390
We did the fitting
without the wig

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:02.880
and he's playing, having a
good time in the fitting.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:10.190
And I just thought it was such a
great costume in process photo.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:14.300
This is from the set from
John Cameron Mitchell wearing

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:17.150
that hair dress that
we spoke about earlier.

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:20.460
This is from the set
of "Wig in the Box,"

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:24.470
which is like a music
video within the film.

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:29.180
It's the one kind of magical
reality fantasy moment

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:32.600
and we actually shot
this, I was reminded,

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:37.690
I think on our last
day of shooting.

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:42.300
And it was-- we were doing it
at twilight and our production

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.760
designer Thérèse
DePrez such a genius,

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:48.660
had rigged this trailer wall
so that it would fall down

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.980
and there are these stage
lights in the corner.

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:54.570
So here we are in
between setups.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:56.310
You can see a fan
in the background

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:59.760
because it was hot as hell in
the summer in Toronto shooting.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:02.880
And John in high
heels, of course,

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:07.050
taking a break patiently
waiting for his next set up.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:11.490
This is the iconic
Hedwig costume.

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:17.760
This costume of repurposed
denim romper and this cape

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.520
that mimics the Berlin Wall.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:24.270
All the art was done by this
incredible artist Miguel

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:25.740
Villalobos.

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:30.360
Hedwig the movie is based on an
Off-Broadway play in New York.

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:33.570
And in the play, Hedwig
only had one costume.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:40.320
I didn't design the play,
but this was the film version

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:45.090
of that recycled denim
costume that was so famous

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:47.070
in the Off-Broadway production.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:52.530
This is a scene when Hedwig is
prostituting in the Meatpacking

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:54.540
District in New York
and Tommy Gnosis

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:56.610
comes in and picks her up.

00:10:56.610 --> 00:11:00.000
This is an on-set
photo that I took.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.550
I was inspired by
a fashion designer

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:05.220
friend of mine,
Jeremy Scott, who

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.910
had done this collection and
this idea of the kind of one

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:12.150
leg trouser and so we
did our Hedwig version.

00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:15.330
So John's wearing
a thigh-high boot

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:17.790
that he wore in the "Wig
in the Box" costume.

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:20.610
And what I would say about
the costumes in general,

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:23.280
is they're "method
costumes" in that,

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:26.160
every costume I
felt that we created

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:29.490
I had to be in the head of
Hedwig of how she would have

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:32.320
created this for herself.

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:37.380
Because she is a DIY character,
do it yourself person.

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:41.292
So that's what this
Polaroid shows,

00:11:41.292 --> 00:11:43.950
a little gold razor blade.

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:49.170
There's a lot of
humor and a wink

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:51.150
and a nod in a lot
of these costumes.

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:53.810
This is this from the set.

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:59.210
This is at the end of the
song, "Exquisite Corpse" where

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:02.300
Hedwig really just--

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:04.250
I made this dress
out of plastic.

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:07.490
Actually, we had to
make multiples of them,

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:11.540
and they were wrapped
literally plastic bags.

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:14.930
And we built up
the plastic so much

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:18.840
that I was able to sew trim
on and I put grommets in it.

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:23.840
And this is a scene
when Hedwig has really

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:30.770
come to a point where she
is tired of the artifice,

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:35.750
and rips her dress
and it's the crescendo

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:39.980
of the end of the film and ends
up leaving the stage naked.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:44.621
So this is the stripping away of
the artifice of this, you know,

00:12:44.621 --> 00:12:46.860
and gender.

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:49.760
This is really an
interesting conversation now

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:51.810
that we're having
culturally about gender.

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:55.310
And this is really about Hedwig
not subscribing to gender

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:59.070
and just being true
to who Hedwig is.

00:12:59.070 --> 00:12:59.700
Aha!

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:01.140
The hair dress.

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:03.630
So my partner in crime
on the hair dress

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:10.290
is I enlisted genius
wig designer and make-up

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:12.090
artist, Mike Potter.

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:15.780
Who created Hedwig, the original
wig and the make-up design

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.840
with John back in
the clubs of New York

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:20.640
before it was an
Off-Broadway show.

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:24.270
And Mike has been part
of Hedwig's evolution

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:29.710
from the clubs of New York
to Off-Broadway to the movie.

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:35.610
And then we all were reunited
on Broadway a few years ago.

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:39.900
So Mike Potter, I
enlisted his expertise

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:43.015
once we sewed these wig wefts.

00:13:43.015 --> 00:13:43.890
They're called wefts.

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:49.770
Wefts of hair, like
a fringe curtain.

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:54.810
And this was a Saturday,
a day off during shooting

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:56.820
in our hotel room in Toronto.

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:01.470
And I think John ended up just
crying in the middle of this,

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:03.420
he was so tired.

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:06.450
But we were listening
to loud music

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:08.325
and giving the
costume a haircut.

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:15.970
This is a scene from set, a
Polaroid on set from the Menses

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:20.380
Fair, which is a
riff on the, I guess,

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:23.530
the Lilith Fair of the 90s.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:27.010
And actually, this
piece was designed

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:31.720
by this really wonderful
independent designer,

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:34.750
fashion designer named Grey Ant.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:39.220
And he was generous
enough to loan this to me

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:40.990
for the shooting of the movie.

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:46.490
And I loved it for its scale
and volume and it's oversized.

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:50.470
It's like a top, you can't
see the rest of the costume.

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:53.710
But it was perfect
for the scene and it

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:58.190
was that was a beautiful
day we were shooting.

00:14:58.190 --> 00:14:58.850
So here we go.

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:01.090
So here's a fitting
where John actually

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:04.300
has a stand-in wig
so we could get

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:08.500
a sense of what the hair would
look like with the costume.

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:13.240
This is a costume in progress
that we made for "Sugar Daddy."

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:17.380
The song "Sugar Daddy"
when Hedwig literally

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:20.650
gives a quote unquote,
"car wash" to a customer

00:15:20.650 --> 00:15:21.940
Bilgewaters.

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:28.450
So there's John in a
very enthusiastic pose

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:31.550
during the fitting.

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:36.740
This is a shot from the
set that I was lucky enough

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:41.120
to get close in on of
Michael Pitt and Hedwig

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:44.900
on set shooting the scene
from the trailer park.

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:46.970
And I particularly
love this Polaroid,

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:48.980
the lighting just
worked out beautifully.

00:15:48.980 --> 00:15:51.710
And there's like a--

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:53.570
I love the expression
on John's face,

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:59.700
there's like a poignancy to
this photo that is so sweet.

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:02.670
And here's from the same
session of them, I guess,

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:03.760
I busted them.

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:06.030
I caught a private
moment and then

00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:08.160
maybe they were rehearsing
in between setups

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:10.740
I can't remember, probably.

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:14.070
And here they are being--

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:15.900
we just had so
much fun on the set

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.870
it was so great to
see these smiles

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:21.480
and be reminded of that
time shooting together,

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:23.870
it was really special.

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:27.653
Here we are again at the
monitors for that "Wig

00:16:27.653 --> 00:16:29.510
in the Box" scene.

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:32.270
John, as the
director, of course,

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:35.670
in full costume with
Frankie DeMarco.

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:37.580
You can see right
next to John who's

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:41.310
the DP the cinematographer,
and they're talking,

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:42.320
I'm sure about the shot.

00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:45.110
Behind John is Miriam
Shor, who plays Yitzhak.

00:16:45.110 --> 00:16:48.500
And behind Miriam in white
you see Stephen Trask,

00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:52.760
who's the lyricist and
co-creator of Hedwig.

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:56.480
And I don't remember the
other people unfortunately,

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:59.990
it's been 20 years but
crew members there.

00:16:59.990 --> 00:17:01.190
So that is on set.

00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:04.819
You can see we have
a pop-up over us.

00:17:04.819 --> 00:17:07.430
I think it was threatening
rain that night,

00:17:07.430 --> 00:17:11.130
a humid Toronto summer.

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:14.640
Here is a shot of
John in between takes

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:18.630
at the beginning of the
song "Exquisite Corpse"

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:21.660
in that plastic dress
which was really

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:25.050
created building up layers
and layers of basically

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:29.530
plastic bags and clear tape.

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:36.240
And then we put grommets and I
sewed in like a beaded fringe

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:37.900
on the bottom.

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:43.480
And this is John's costume for
"Exquisite Corpse," of course.

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:46.890
We saw in the other Polaroid
when he rips it off.

00:17:46.890 --> 00:17:53.640
It's the crescendo of
"Exquisite Corpse."

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:59.760
This is just an artistic shot
I did on set with the cape

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:03.045
from the opening costume
from "Tear Me Down."

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:12.180
John is so photogenic and
is such a brilliant actor,

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:14.010
let alone director.

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:16.080
And a muse really for me.

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:18.360
I took a lot of
pictures of John on set

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:21.960
because I thought he was
just so beautiful as Hedwig

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:24.840
and I was so moved
by his ability

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:27.930
to seamlessly move in front
and behind the camera,

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:30.700
so incredible.

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:33.160
Here we are in
that "Sugar Daddy"

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:39.520
costume behind the camera
probably getting a wig touch-up

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:42.880
as John always as
people know on set,

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:44.500
actors with a lot
of make-up have

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:47.020
to drink water with straws.

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:52.240
And just an in-between moment
on the set from shooting the car

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:57.170
wash scene, the
"Sugar Daddy" scene.

00:18:57.170 --> 00:18:58.760
This might have
been a continuity

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:01.730
photo where I was trying to--

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:04.190
but maybe it was just
an artistic shot.

00:19:04.190 --> 00:19:08.930
That wig always makes for such
a great frame on John's face.

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:10.910
So you can see the
costumes close up.

00:19:10.910 --> 00:19:17.690
There's a little rabbit fur pull
on there and lots of crystals,

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:20.240
I always say I was my own
sweat shop on this movie.

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:24.320
There are only two of us in our
department, sometimes three.

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:28.520
And so it was actually a
really wonderful experience

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:30.440
having just come
off a big studio

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:32.660
film to work on an
independent film

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:35.450
where I was doing
everything and that

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:39.660
really helped me kind of
re-calibrate how important it

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:40.160
is.

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:44.480
Sometimes we get removed from
the set as costume designers

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:45.710
since we're always--

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:48.110
we're technically
an off-set position,

00:19:48.110 --> 00:19:51.200
or we get removed from
the making of a costume.

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:55.340
So this is such a
memorable film for me

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:59.210
because I was able
to be involved

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:06.560
in all aspects of the costumes
from creation to being on-set.

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:11.270
And this is Mike Potter, the
genius wig designer and make-up

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:14.630
artist who created
Hedwig's look from day one.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:18.290
From the nightclubs of New York
City to Off-Broadway Hedwig

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:19.850
to the film.

00:20:19.850 --> 00:20:25.970
He was my partner in
crime and we were--

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:28.370
the costume is
really only completed

00:20:28.370 --> 00:20:29.610
by the wig and make-up.

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:31.480
And we really, really
worked closely.

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:36.140
I don't think I've ever worked
so intimately with another hair

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:37.940
and make-up artist
so that was really

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:42.650
thrilling that we were able
to create these looks together

00:20:42.650 --> 00:20:44.660
with different wigs.

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:46.280
That's Mike again.

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:52.490
Mike Potter who played a little
guest role as a prostitute

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:56.240
in the Meatpacking
District, a male prostitute.

00:20:56.240 --> 00:21:00.110
Male drag prostitute in
the Meatpacking District.

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:06.360
But I think he's in
partial make-up there.

00:21:06.360 --> 00:21:09.000
And Mike Potter again.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:13.740
He is modeling the
Hedwig's sunglasses

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:16.320
that we see in the
beginning of the movie.

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:19.590
He was my muse as well.

00:21:19.590 --> 00:21:23.610
And Mike Potter doing the hair
for one of the "hed heads,"

00:21:23.610 --> 00:21:25.790
which are Hedwig's groupies.

00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:31.460
This is Paul who is actually a
longtime friend of John Cameron

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:32.390
Mitchell.

00:21:32.390 --> 00:21:34.880
John had a lot of his
friends play different parts

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:38.520
in the movie, it was a
real family atmosphere.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:44.730
This is Michael Pitt in a
fitting for Tommy Gnosis, one

00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:48.030
of his performance costumes.

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:51.410
And Michael was
incredible to work with.

00:21:51.410 --> 00:21:54.530
Another Tommy Gnosis
costume fitting

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:57.977
with Michael Pitt
and another one.

00:21:57.977 --> 00:21:59.310
I don't think that made the cut.

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:03.870
And this is a photo
of Michael Pitt

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:07.110
on set for that
trailer park scene

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:11.280
and I love the lighting out.

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:13.980
It's just by
chance, by accident,

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:16.830
that he's out of
focus and you get--

00:22:16.830 --> 00:22:21.060
you can see the cables in
the right corner of the set

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:23.100
and then you can
see that painted

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:25.270
backdrop in the background.

00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:30.460
So you really get a sense
of the movie-making aspect.

00:22:30.460 --> 00:22:36.240
And that's him as young Tommy
before it becomes Tommy Gnosis.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:40.940
And Michael Pitt
as Tommy Gnosis,

00:22:40.940 --> 00:22:44.610
I believe that's on set because
that's a costume t-shirt.

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:46.890
I don't think it's a fitting.

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:54.880
Probably in-between takes and
probably rehearsing guitar.

00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:58.010
He did a lot of
rehearsing guitar.

00:22:58.010 --> 00:23:00.230
And there's Michael
as Tommy Gnosis

00:23:00.230 --> 00:23:03.080
with these fabulous
leather trousers

00:23:03.080 --> 00:23:10.220
we made for him, as low as we
could go in that Jim Morrison

00:23:10.220 --> 00:23:11.270
tradition.

00:23:11.270 --> 00:23:14.030
Wanted these leather
pants to feel

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:16.370
like a second skin and the
way that Frankie DeMarco was

00:23:16.370 --> 00:23:18.615
shooting it was
in very low light.

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:24.020
You also see Hedwig in
this scene coming out

00:23:24.020 --> 00:23:29.490
of darkness so I'm very happy
with the way it turned out.

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:32.720
Well, that was really fantastic
Arianne, thank you so much.

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:35.400
It is great to feel
like we were on the set

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:39.540
and at the same time to be able
to look at the detail on some

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:40.350
of these Polaroids.

00:23:40.350 --> 00:23:42.140
When you're watching
a film sometimes

00:23:42.140 --> 00:23:44.640
the costumes are so busy moving
around that you don't really

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:47.640
get to see it so this
was a really fun way

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:50.070
to concentrate a little
bit more on your work

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:54.960
and at the same time celebrate
what was actually going

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.720
on 20 years ago, as you were
making the film, so thank you

00:23:57.720 --> 00:23:58.620
so much.

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:01.500
Thank you so much for having me.

